// ============================================================================
//  Strings.def – EVERY user-visible text of the program, in all languages
// ============================================================================
//
//  One row per text:   X(Id,  "Estonian",  "English",  "Japanese")
//
//  This file is included TWICE by core/I18n.cpp/.hpp, each time with a different
//  definition of X (the "X-macro" technique): once to make the enum `Str::Id`,
//  once to make the table of texts. So a row can never be added to one list and
//  forgotten in the other, and all three languages of a text sit on one line, so a
//  missing translation is visible at a glance (and a unit test fails if Estonian or
//  English is empty).
//
//  Rules
//   * An EMPTY "" means "not translated yet": tr() falls back to English.
//   * {name} is a placeholder, filled by i18n::fmt(Str::Id, {{"name", value}}).
//     Word order differs between languages, so never glue sentences from pieces
//     in code; give the translator the whole sentence with its placeholders.
//     Every language must use the same placeholders (a unit test checks).
//   * \n is a line break (message boxes only).
//   * Japanese text avoids "~" and "\" : the Japanese font draws byte 0x7E as an
//     overline and 0x5C as a yen sign (JIS X 0201), see docs/03-text-and-reader.md.
//   * NOT in this file, on purpose: log lines and error texts from the system
//     (they should look the same in every bug report), file names, settings keys, the
//     JMdict licence acknowledgement (its wording comes from the EDRDG and stays
//     English).
//   * Estonian and Japanese here were written by the assistant and need a native
//     speaker's review. English is the reference.
//
//  The language order (Estonian, English, Japanese) is the order of `enum Lang`.
// ============================================================================

